Q: Export HTML table data to Excel using JavaScript / jQuery is not working properly in Chrome browser A: <p>You can use <em>tableToExcel.js</em> to export a table in an Excel file.</p>
<p>This works in the following way:</p>
<p>1). <strong>Include this CDN in your project/file</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/linways/table-to-excel@v1.0.4/dist/tableToExcel.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>2). <strong>Either using JavaScript:</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;button id=&quot;btnExport&quot; onclick=&quot;exportReportToExcel(this)&quot;&gt;EXPORT REPORT&lt;/button&gt;

function exportReportToExcel() {
  let table = document.getElementsByTagName(&quot;table&quot;); // You can use document.getElementById('tableId') as well by providing id to the table tag
  TableToExcel.convert(table[0], { // HTML code may contain multiple tables, so here we are referring to the first table tag
    name: `export.xlsx`, // The filename. You could use any name
    sheet: {
      name: 'Sheet 1' // Sheet name
    }
  });
}
</code></pre>
<p>3). <strong>Or by using <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JQuery" rel="nofollow noreferrer">jQuery</a></strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;button id=&quot;btnExport&quot;&gt;EXPORT REPORT&lt;/button&gt;

$(document).ready(function() {
    $(&quot;#btnExport&quot;).click(function() {
        let table = document.getElementsByTagName(&quot;table&quot;);
        TableToExcel.convert(table[0], { // HTML code may contain multiple tables, so here we are referring to the first table tag
           name: `export.xlsx`, // The filename. You could use any name
           sheet: {
              name: 'Sheet 1' // Sheet name
           }
        });
    });
});
</code></pre>
<p>You may refer to this GitHub link for other information:</p>
<p><em><a href="https://github.com/linways/table-to-excel/tree/master" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Table to Excel 2</a></em></p>
<p>Or <a href="https://codepen.io/rohithb/pen/YdjVbb" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the live example</a>.</p>

Q: Having issue with my state not appearing on my first render until I refresh A: <p><code>useState()</code> only uses its initializer on the first render.</p>
<p>Most likely, during client-side navigation <code>product</code> is initially missing/old and arrives later, so this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const [imageChanges, setImageChanges] = useState(
    mode === &quot;update&quot; &amp;&amp; product
        ? product.product_images.map(...)
        : []
)
</code></pre>
<p>initializes to <code>[]</code> and is never updated. On refresh, <code>product</code> is already available on the first render, which explains why the images appear.</p>
<p>Sync the image state when the product changes:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (mode === &quot;update&quot; &amp;&amp; product) {
        setImageChanges(
            product.product_images.map(img =&gt; ({
                type: &quot;db&quot;,
                image: { ...img, isDeleted: false },
            }))
        )
    }
}, [mode, product])
</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, if each product form should have completely fresh state, key it by product id:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;GlobalProductForm
    key={product.id}
    product={product}
    ...
/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Also remove this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>useLayoutEffect(() =&gt; {
    return () =&gt; setImageChanges([])
}, [])
</code></pre>
<p>Setting state while the component is unmounting is unnecessary and can cause its own problems.</p>
